l this  political activity to take care of his  grandmother or attend an evening class  or did my father overhear a word phrase  a sentence an appeal for justice that  made him hesitate linger and join the  throngs of people unrest and strikes  across the island that continued  throughout the month of May were met by  force in addition to the police there  were six platoons of the Sherwood  Foresters Regiment of the British Army  almost five thousand armed special  constables and two cruisers of the Royal  Navy by May 23rd a general strike was  underway in Kingston kaol would have  gone to work as usual but as strikers  and protesters marched down King Street  toward parade early in the day all the  stores closed  the city was brought to a standstill a  strikes of manual workers and train  drivers erupted throughout the capital  what did my father witness in 1938 that  made him so afraid for the safety of his  daughter marching in London decades  later what memories of these events  still haunted him in the last months of  his life lessons that he wanted his  daughter to know and to pass on on the  first day of the Kingston strike police  beat protesters with their battles by  the next day May 24th  Empire Day a public holiday military  trucks patrolled Kingston and the  police were using bullets that killed  and wounded civilians residents of  Kingston had to face 400 police officers  80 troops 250 special constables and the  Kingston infantry volunteers in cars  neighbourhood a woman and her  nine-year-old son were killed by police  in Matthew Lane both of them just shot  in the head the police clubbed anyone  who gathered around to watch on Hanover  Street a youth was bayonetted in the leg  and on North Street two girls and a man  was shot by military paroles patrols  this violence continued and increased  and on May 25th Kingston shop assistants  and clans joined the strikers the  British government more concerned with  their Imperial adventures on the  continent of Africa and in India  relegated their crown colony of Jamaica  to the status and I quote from records  an imperial backwater  and regarded it as quote a financial  burden poverty and pain were constant  companions in Jamaica decades of  colonial neglect and the starvation  wages and malnutrition inevitably  followed lay not just on the surface of  the skin in the beaded sweat of rebels  but hollowed out the bodies of adults  and children softening bones in flaming  joints penetrating livers spleens  kidneys and hearts attacking lungs and  bowels the effects of colonial neglect  can be seen in the high death rate among  infants and it's also evident in the  diseases that were suffered by many  members of Carl's family Murray's health  deteriorated and she developed  difficulty breathing a doctor  recommended that they move closer to the  water where the sea air might help and  Carl became desperate and searched for  weeks to find somewhere on or near the  seafront that he could afford eventually  moving with his grandmother to twelve  water lane where they shared a house  with other tenants poor working people  always have to work multiple jobs and  Carl for Carl it was no different he was  employed as the caretaker for water lane  in return for the reduct for a reduction  in rent he was responsible for the other  talents for the house for the grounds  and for maintaining its Beach so  becoming caretaker was added to his  full-time job attending evening classes  helping younger siblings with their  schoolwork and nursing his grandmother  I suspect Marie suffered from pulmonary  tuberculosis my father always became  deliberately vague when asked about his  grandmother's symptoms as if he had to  conceal what he knew and felt there was  such a stigma attached to the disease  that he would have been ashamed to admit  to it if it was true now in the  Metropole the 1911 national insurance  Act and the public health acts of 1921  and 1936 actually laid the groundwork  for legislation that would provide a  National Health Service after World War  two and in the UK there was a complex  network of sanatoria hospitals  dispensaries and medical specialists  devoted to the free treatment of  tuberculosis that was built through  direct state intervention in Jamaica  though the British state abdicated  responsibility colonial subjects had to  pay a fee for each use of the  underfunded and frequently inadequate  medical services there was a system of  medical relief tickets for subjects  earning less than twelve shillings a  week but to be eligible one had to prove  that you were on the poor per roll or  deemed worthy by a parochial board  responsible for the distribution of  tickets and in addition to that a  separate ticket had to be obtained for  each and every medical visit the ticket  system became the most criticized aspect  of the colony's medical services Carl  was able to ease the final days weeks  and months of Murray's life but he could  not prevent her death on the 6th of May  in 1941  my father's stories and memories  revealed the emergence of a man whose  sense of self coalesced around the  concept of dignity a dignity maintained  in relation to conditions of personhood  deportment and employment  wearing shirts with a collar and a tie a  suit polished shoes working in a clean  and orderly environment being trusted to  tend to the accounts of a business  becoming a person who could gain and  retain respect in the face of desperate  poverty my father adopted the clerical  garb and manners of accountancy as a  counterweight to the terrible conditions  his clothes were part of keeping it  together a demonstration of restraint  and dignity as he buttoned his collar  and knotted his tie my father swallowed  the bile of poverty and kept in check  the emotions that surged when he was  being treated as less than human the  immediate effect of the outbreak of war  in September 1939 was that the prices of  food firewood kerosene men's trousers  boots and imported cloth soared although  no one escaped the effect of rampant  inflation this was a disaster  for the poor who were barely surviving  even before the price increases by 1942  Jamaica was in a dire economic crisis  with massive unemployment no fuel was  being delivered because German u-boats  were operating throughout the Caribbean  destroying shipping and oil storage  tanks crime in Kingston rose  dramatically one ironic comment on the  state of wartime Jamaica was made by  street gangs  who chose to operate under the name of  RAF conjuring the image of the Royal Air  Force but actually meaning Rob and free  recruitment for the Royal Air Force  began in Jamaica in April of 1942 and my  father was always proud to say that he  was among the first to volunteer after a  series of rigorous and medical  educational tests Carr was accepted into  the RAF to train as aircrew he made a  special point of saying that he was  issued with two sets of tropical uniform  I never understood the significance of  this remark until I discovered how  extremely expensive clothes had become  in wartime Jamaica car left the island  on June 18th 1943 unaware that he would  never live there again it was a full  week journey the first leg of which was  on a Canadian steamship to New York and  then via Canadian Pacific rail to  Moncton New Brunswick it was not at home  but at school that the child who was to  become this Airman's daughter was told  that Britain in 1940 stood alone facing  invasion after the fall of Europe to  Hitler's armies and the evacuation of  the British Expeditionary Force from  Dunkirk in school many of the speeches  of Winston Churchill were read out loud  by the teachers and poetic phrases  became ingrained in the minds of the  pupils who in turn were forced to recite  and now it has come to us to stand alone  in the breach and face the worst that  the tyrants might  enmity can do we are fighting by  ourselves alone but we are not fighting  for ourselves alone Churchill's words  were used as a pedagogical entre to  instill British character the preeminent  qualities of which we were told were  manifest during the war his phrases and  his cadence were resonant with  Shakespearean language meter and imagery  particularly the speeches of Henry the  fifth which we were also taught to  recite the question that dogged me as a  schoolgirl was how the peculiarities of  English character evident during World  War two when they stood alone related to  the framed portrait of my father in his  RAF uniform which stood on our piano  even if he could be characterized as a  good yeoman his limbs were not made in  England I was unable to solve the  contradiction when only a girl but it  was my first lesson in historical  amnesia it was not for many years that I  understood that a might of the British  Empire and its powerful Imperial economy  rendered the meaning of standing alone  and absurd if efficacious claim Britain  mobilised vast resources including  people from around the globe and  assembled them into one of the most for  machines the planet has ever seen the  RAF smore time expansion was embodied in  the British Commonwealth air training  plan the most ambitious aviation  training programme in history my  father's amazement was evident in his  voice when he described arriving  arriving in Moncton as like a holiday  what he remembered most vividly was the  food lots of excellent meals and as much  as one could devour my father was no  longer hungry  Carl began the next stage of his journey  in Halifax Nova Scotia where he and  other RAF recruits from the Caribbean  joined a large North Atlantic convoy  consisting of cargo vessels protected by  battleships and frigates recruits were  packed for to a bunk on a troop ship it  was like pushing animals together  because they really have the ships all  cramped to make sure they got as many as  they put to fight for England it was in  this convoy that Carl entered the Battle  of and for the Atlantic I was only able  to extract very thin threads of memory  from my father which easily became  tangled and only gradually could I  loosen them actually in the archive of  the Admiralty records where the calculus  of war and the brutal reasonings of  expendability became evident in his  usual restrained and very understated  manner my father described his journey  across the Atlantic as being quote more  eventful than life at Moncton because  the convoy was  attacked by German submarines sound  travels underwater much faster than  through the air so I realized that he  and the other recruits lying in the  bowels of the ship probably seasick  would have heard the explosions before  the crew on the deck shock waves would  have rocked the ship was this the time  and place I wondered when car began to  feel fear when the feeling of adventure  the holiday ended and war began my  father told me he did not know until he  landed in the UK that two of the ships  in the convoy were lost in the attack  but he never spoke about what happened  to the crew aboard those ships such  silences distance myself from disaster  and from danger as does the language of  war it was ships that were lost not  human beings  my father's careful word choice rendered  invisible the fact that 186 people died  and dozens were wounded when the or can  flying under the flag of Poland was  torpedoed and sank within five minutes  on October the eighth and a cargo vessel  the u.s. York Mar was torpedoed and sank  the following day what Kyle did not know  what he could not have known what none  of them who made that journey knew or  were ever to know was that their convoy  SC 143 was used as bait in the battle  for the control of the North Atlantic  the British enigma cipher machine had  broken the German codes and the Western  Approaches command knew that rossbach a  wolf pack of  new boats was lying in wait for SC 143  so they seize the opportunity to attack  it the convoy was reinforced with four  destroyers including the Orkin which  sank and the entire convoy was sent  directly towards Ross back but it was  not only these calculations of  expendability that I had to discover in  the archives  I knew that my father's training in the  RAF involved not only the acquisition of  skills that he described in detail  flying wireless telegraphy military  drills but that there were multiple  realities and equations of war that he  did not pass on he was silent about his  role in a war machine that was  determined to exercise its maximum  destructive capacity no longer a clerk  in the backroom of tailors notion and  hardware and furniture dealer whose  horizons were confined by the columns of  account books and the endless battles to  fend off despair and destitution for his  family Karl mapped a space for his  existence that must have appeared vast  with possibility the wages and apparent  benefits of the war machine are a denial  of its fundamental inhumanity if my  father was scarred by what he saw of the  violence of the Imperial state  perpetrated against people just like him  on the streets of Kingston if he found  such violence terrible he had nothing to  say  about the Allied policy of massacre by  aerial bombardment that was perpetrated  by the RAF on German cities my father  and his crew served in Bomber Command  twentieth squadron when they were flying  information among a fleet of advancing  bombers I wonder if my father and his  crew ever saw themselves as WG Seybold  has described as teeming messengers of  death striking like the apocalypse  did they ever attempt to account for the  destruction that they unleashed or  recognize that the war in the air was  pure and undisguised when I first heard  about Bomber Command and about Essen  described by my father as an industrial  town I imagined the destruction of  factories and machinery I was unable to  comprehend the full horror the  deliberate annihilation of people their  dwellings their history and their  natural environment I do not know what  my father thought that he and his crew  and his squadron left behind them in  Essen after they had dropped their bombs  when my father recounted his most  difficult and painful experience of the  war it was the breakup of his crew and  their demobilization I will always  remember his words after spending so  much time together and sharing various  experiences as one a very strong bond  developed and breaking off this bond  suddenly  was far from being easy he treasured  being as one and for him for the first  time in his life that meant being  regarded as an absolute equal my father  was mistaken to think that such  camaraderie once forged would last  forever because what he was to face in  post-war Britain were times more  difficult than he could possibly have  imagined thank you  [Applause]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

